{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Research an efficient sequential dense-DP implementation in src/solution.cpp. Replace two DP rows and the row-copy loop with one in-place row: initialize dp[0]=1, then process every cell row-major, setting blocked cells to zero and otherwise adding the current above value to the updated left value. Replace remainder with conditional subtraction; two residues sum to at most 2000000012, which fits signed 32-bit arithmetic. Investigate buffered input and row-indexed obstacle storage, processing obstacle-free intervals without a per-cell obstacle lookup. Avoid an unconditional quadratic allocation; preserve duplicate-obstacle semantics. Measure changes separately and together. Maintain correctness for n=1, blocked endpoints, boundaries, and all legal obstacle patterns.\",\n  \"B\": \"Research a sparse combinatorial alternative. Deduplicate blocked coordinates, sort them topologically by coordinates, and precompute factorials/inverse factorials through 2*n-2 modulo 1000000007. For each blocked point, calculate unrestricted paths from the origin minus paths whose first blocked point is an earlier coordinate-wise predecessor. Apply the same subtraction to the destination to obtain the answer. This costs O(n+u^2) time and O(n+u) storage for u unique obstacles, so it must not replace dense DP unconditionally. Include the general empty-obstacle binomial case and immediate zero for blocked endpoints. Develop a conservative algorithmic-work crossover with a correct dense fallback; document preprocessing costs and measured crossover evidence rather than assuming sparsity from the statement.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research four-core CPU parallelism for dense cases using tiled wavefront DP. Execute independent tiles on the same tile anti-diagonal concurrently, retaining the original recurrence inside each tile. Pass top and left boundary vectors between successive wavefronts; use rolling boundary frontiers and per-worker scratch rather than an n-by-n DP allocation. Associate obstacles with tiles while preserving duplicates as one blocked cell. Derive ownership, corner indexing, boundary lifetime, and synchronization carefully. Explore a small range of tile sizes and serial fallback thresholds under the existing build configuration. Measure synchronization and preprocessing overhead against A-style sequential DP; the approximately 81 ms baseline makes overhead significant. Do not assume additional compiler options, libraries, GPUs, or more than four cores.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate only correctness-validated programs produced by A, B, and C, retaining the best measured standalone candidate as a fallback. First evaluate A's sequential improvements; then assess B's sparse method with a work-based dense fallback; finally assess C's parallel dense engine independently and in combination. Shared parsing and obstacle representations may change crossover behavior, so remeasure every integrated program rather than adding reported gains. Prefer a simple implementation if differences are within timing variation. Use only the existing compiler, target, tests, oracle, and budgets. Preserve stdin/stdout behavior and correctness for every valid input; never dispatch on test identities or expected outputs.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The supplied source implements Problem D, grid-path counting, not the other problems in the booklet. It performs quadratic DP, stores a quadratic packed obstacle grid, copies a DP row after every iteration, and uses formatted input. The only measured program is correct on four existing cases, with a stable mean of 0.081009 seconds; no workload dimensions or comparative optimization results are available. Accordingly, A targets low-overhead recurrence and storage improvements, B targets an algorithmic improvement when obstacles are sufficiently few, and C explores bounded-memory parallel dense computation on the four-core ARM64 target. Sparse inclusion-exclusion can be much worse for many obstacles, and parallel wavefront overhead can outweigh its benefits on short runs. All paths count modulo 1000000007 and must support the full stated input domain without quadratic-memory assumptions.\"\n}"}}
